3. ORDINARY DEVELOPMENT AUTHORITY

Within an effective task or campaign, competent sections may direct and execute ordinary authorized non-production development.

This includes:

* Development branch creation.
* Authorized repository file edits.
* Commits.
* Testing and validation.
* Bounded technical repairs.
* Pull request creation and updates.
* Review coordination.
* Technical integration preparation.
* Technical currentness reconciliation.

Routine implementation choices within approved requirements and scope belong to the competent professional section.

A new personal Commander decision shall not be required solely because another ordinary technical development step is performed.

Existing requirements for task authority, scope, technical-account authorization, provenance, testing, review and protected-operation controls remain applicable.

⸻

4. DEVELOPMENT-TOOL INDEPENDENCE

ChatGPT, Codex, Claude, Lovable and other qualified development environments may be used interchangeably within the same effective development task.

Changing the AI model, conversation, session or development environment does not by itself require:

* A new Commander decision.
* A new campaign activation.
* A new individual AI-session authorization.
* A new task authorization.
* A new Build Hold exception.

This applies where the authorized mission, task, scope, responsible function, technical account and applicable validity conditions remain unchanged.

The previously effective global AI execution simplification remains preserved.

This decision consolidates its operational consequence.

Authenticated technical-account requirements, provider-attributable provenance and protected-operation execution controls remain applicable.

4.1 Prospective supersession

Historical general execution restrictions that solely require a particular AI model, conversation, session or development-tool instance are prospectively superseded within the effective ordinary development envelope.

This includes requirements for repeated Commander approval solely because the development tool changes.

This supersession does not remove:

* Authenticated technical-account requirements.
* Repository and file-scope restrictions.
* Required provider-attributable provenance.
* Protected-operation identity requirements.
* Explicit security restrictions.
* Valid single-use mutation controls.
* Existing expiry and revocation.
* Required independent review.
* Exact-object controls where applicable.

An earlier task-specific instrument that expressly requires a particular application, conversation or technical actor as a substantive security, provenance or protected-execution condition remains effective until competently changed.

A special authorization shall not be treated as a universal restriction on ordinary development.

No completed or consumed authorization is retroactively changed.

